EXT. SELF-SERVE CAR WASH - DUSK

A concrete bay, wet and humming. Neon over the coin box. Foam slides down a windshield.

PILAR MOSQUERA, 30s, in a rain jacket, holds a pressure wand in one hand and her phone in the other. She is rinsing a green hatchback with great care.

PILAR
(into phone, light)
No, it's fine. I'm rinsing. It's very dramatic.

ANTON (V.O.)
(warm, over the phone)
Pilar, you sound happy.

PILAR
I am happy. Is that suspicious?

ANTON (V.O.)
It's a little suspicious.

She laughs. She sprays the back bumper, then the tires.

PILAR
Did you get the table?

ANTON (V.O.)
Seven-thirty. The one by the window.

PILAR
You remembered I like the window.

ANTON (V.O.)
I remember everything.

Her smile holds a half beat too long. She turns off the wand. Water drips.

PILAR
Anton. The window table. That's where you proposed to Rosa.

A long silence on the line. A distant horn.

ANTON (V.O.)
I thought it'd be nice.

PILAR
It would be nice. For her.

She opens the car door, slides into the driver's seat, and sets the phone on the dashboard. Foam slides off the windshield and the world goes bright white.

PILAR (CONT'D)
(into phone)
I'll see you at seven-thirty.
